  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 226 PM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nBeginning Wednesday, a weak ridge should begin to develop over the \ncentral plains, slowly propagating to the east through Saturday. \nEnsemble guidance is in general agreement on the ridge axis \npositioning to the east of our county warning area (CWA) which \nshould give us upper level flow from the southwest as we head into \nnext weekend. Higher confidence exists at the moment of this ridge \nweakening through Saturday, bringing zonal westerly flow aloft to \nthe region during the weekend. A weak upper-level jet streak should \nbe in place Wednesday, ushering in breezy conditions to the area. \nConfidence is medium that this feature will move out of the area by \nThursday. At the surface, weak surface low pressure is forecasted to \ninfluence the area through Friday. Simultaneously, a frontal \nboundary moves through the area Wednesday which should give us \nboosted chances for showers and thunderstorms. Chances for \nprecipitation the rest of the week will be dependent on shortwave \npassages through the ridging system.\n\nWith respect to Probability of Precipitation (PoPs), forecast \nguidance displays 25-40% Wednesday during frontal passage. Eastern \nportions of the CWA may see up to 50% PoPs overnight into Thursday \nmorning as the front moves off to the east. Heading towards the \nweekend, 15-30% PoPs for Thursday as well as 25-45% Friday are \nhighlighted by guidance. 15-30% is the current probability range \nSaturday as the overall pattern becomes zonal. Instability and bulk \nshear are forecasted to be low during this long-term period, making \nany chance for precipitation limited to showers and thunderstorms.\n\nHigh temperatures are expected to be near the seasonal average for \nthis time of the year for the Tri-State area heading into the \nweekend. Highs in the upper 70/low 80s are anticipated for the \nentire four-day forecast period. Surface winds should start off \nbreezy on Wednesday at around 15-25 mph in the eastern and central \nparts of our CWA and 25-30 mph in eastern Colorado, gusting up to 40 \nmph. Winds should die down to a general 5-10 mph Thursday \naccompanied by gusts up to 15 mph. Finally winds likely bounce back \na bit to 10-20 mph Friday and Saturday with gusting up to 25\nmph.",
